---
import {
  Alert02Icon,
  FileSearchIcon,
  Link01Icon,
  Notification01Icon,
  Search01Icon,
  ViewOffIcon,
} from '@hugeicons/core-free-icons'
import CodeBlock from '@/components/CodeBlock.astro'
import FeaturePage from '@/components/FeaturePage.astro'

const related = [
  { title: 'seo index', text: "Google's indexing reason for every known page, each checked live and labelled.", href: '/docs/indexing', icon: Alert02Icon },
  { title: 'seo page', text: 'One URL: what it serves now, its Google index status and its queries.', href: '/docs/page', icon: FileSearchIcon },
  { title: 'Crawled, currently not indexed', text: 'Pages Google fetched and chose not to index.', href: '/features/crawled-currently-not-indexed', icon: ViewOffIcon, link: 'Crawled, not indexed' },
  { title: 'Website not showing up on Google', text: 'Every indexing reason across your site, and what to check for each.', href: '/features/website-not-showing-up-on-google', icon: Search01Icon, link: 'Website not on Google' },
  { title: 'seo internal-links', text: 'Find the pages on your site that should link to one page.', href: '/docs/internal-links', icon: Link01Icon },
  { title: 'Weekly site audit', text: 'Daily Google index checks and an email when a page drops out.', href: '/docs/weekly-site-audit', icon: Notification01Icon },
]

const example = `URLs labelled fix or check, 1 to 3 of 3:
reason | label | url | live | in_sitemap | last_crawl | why
Discovered - currently not indexed | check | /guides/composting | 200 | yes | | Google knows the URL but has not crawled it yet. The sitemap lists it. It returns 200 now.
Discovered - currently not indexed | check | /shop?colour=red&size=9 | 200 | no | | Google knows the URL but has not crawled it yet. It returns 200 now.
Discovered - currently not indexed | fix | /guides/old-compost-bins | 404 | yes | | It returns 404 now, and the sitemap lists it.`
---

<FeaturePage
  title="Discovered, currently not indexed"
  metaTitle="Discovered, currently not indexed: check every page"
  description="Find every page Google knows about but has not crawled, with a live check of each one, and see whether the hold-up is the page, your server or your links."
  path="/features/discovered-currently-not-indexed"
  lead="See every page Google knows about but has not crawled yet. Your agent reads Google's reason for each one and checks what the page does now, so you can tell a broken or unwanted URL from a good page Google has not got to."
  {related}
>
  <h2>What discovered, currently not indexed means</h2>
  <p>
    Discovered, currently not indexed is a reason in Search Console's Page
    indexing report. Google found the URL, through a link or your sitemap,
    and has not crawled it yet.
  </p>
  <p>
    Google's <a href="https://support.google.com/webmasters/answer/7440203" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Page
    indexing report help</a> says that typically Google wanted to crawl the
    URL but expected it to overload the site, so it rescheduled the crawl.
    That is also why the last crawl date is empty.
  </p>
  <p>
    Google has not read the page, so rewriting its text is not the first
    thing to try. Check that the URL works, that you want it indexed, and
    that Google can reach it easily.
  </p>

  <h2>Find every discovered, not indexed page on your site</h2>
  <p>
    Search Console's report shows up to 1,000 example URLs for each reason.
    SEO Skill asks Google's URL Inspection about each page it knows from
    your Search Console and your sitemap, and sorts them by reason.
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      Connect the hosted MCP server and sign in with Google, or run{' '}
      <code>npm i -g seo</code> then <code>seo start</code>. Both are free.
      The site has to be a Search Console property your Google account can
      read.
    </li>
    <li>Ask your agent:</li>
  </ol>
  <CodeBlock value={`Which pages on example.com are discovered but not indexed? Group them by folder and tell me which ones we want in Google.`} wrap />
  <p>Or run it in a terminal:</p>
  <CodeBlock value={`seo index --site example.com`} />
  <p>
    Each run checks 100 pages, in a fixed order: pages that lost their
    clicks, pages by clicks and impressions, sitemap pages, then the rest.
    On your own machine, <code>--max-urls</code> takes it up to the 2,000
    checks Google allows each property a day. The result always says how
    many pages it checked out of how many it knows.
  </p>

  <h2>What you get for a page Google has not crawled</h2>
  <p>
    Google has no crawl of these pages, so its side of the answer is mostly
    empty. The live check fills in the rest: the page's status, any
    redirect, its canonical and robots tags, and whether your sitemap lists
    it. A page that loads is labelled check. A missing page your sitemap
    still lists is labelled fix. Here is the kind of result to expect, for
    example.com:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock value={example} wrap />
  <p>
    Each of the three rows needs something different. The guide is a real
    page that Google has not crawled yet. The filter URL is one you probably never wanted in Google.
    The old guide is gone, and the sitemap still sends Google to it.
  </p>

  <h2>Four things to check, in this order</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      Check the URL works. A page that returns 404, redirects or
      carries a noindex tag now will not be indexed when Google does crawl
      it. Fix the sitemap or the link that sends Google there instead.
    </li>
    <li>
      Decide whether you want it indexed. Filter, sort and tracking URLs
      add to Google's crawl queue without adding a page anyone searches
      for. Take them out of your sitemap and stop linking to them.
    </li>
    <li>
      Check your server answers quickly. Google puts off crawls it
      expects to overload the site. Search Console's{' '}
      <a href="https://support.google.com/webmasters/answer/9679690" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Crawl
      stats report</a> shows how fast your server answered Googlebot and any
      host problems.
    </li>
    <li>
      Make sure your own pages link to it. A page that only your
      sitemap mentions is easy to put off. <code>seo internal-links --url
      https://example.com/guides/composting</code> crawls your site from
      your machine and returns up to eight pages that should link to it.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Look at the pattern before the pages. Hundreds of discovered URLs in one
    folder usually trace back to that folder as a whole, such as a filter
    system or a batch you published at once.
  </p>
  <p>
    None of these checks makes Google crawl or index a page. They remove the
    reasons on your side that Google could have.
  </p>

  <h2>See whether Google has crawled it since</h2>
  <p>Check one URL on its own:</p>
  <CodeBlock value={`seo page --url https://example.com/guides/composting`} wrap />
  <p>
    Once Google crawls the page, URL Inspection shows a last crawl date and
    the reason changes. <code>seo</code> cannot submit URLs to Google. To
    ask for a crawl, use Request indexing in Search Console's{' '}
    <a href="https://support.google.com/webmasters/answer/9012289" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">URL
    Inspection tool</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    A live index check searches Google itself for <code>site:</code> plus
    the URL and an exact phrase from the page, on desktop and mobile. It
    needs a DataForSEO key and costs about 2 to 4 cents a page:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock value={`seo research index_check --target https://example.com/guides/composting`} wrap />

  <h2>No discovered pages on seoskill.dev on 11 November 2026</h2>
  <p>
    When we ran <code>seo index</code> on this site on 11 November 2026, none
    of its 272 known URLs had this reason, which is why the example above
    uses example.com. Three URLs were unknown to Google, a step before
    discovered: Google had no record of them at all. The{' '}
    <a href="/features/website-not-showing-up-on-google">website not showing
    up on Google</a> page covers that reason and the others.
  </p>

  <h2>Discovered or crawled, currently not indexed</h2>
  <p>
    Discovered means Google has the URL and has not fetched it. <a href="/features/crawled-currently-not-indexed">Crawled,
    currently not indexed</a> means Google fetched it and left it out, and
    URL Inspection shows when. A page can move from discovered to crawled,
    so watch for the reason changing.
  </p>

  <h2>Daily index checks with alerts</h2>
  <p>
    The <a href="/docs/weekly-site-audit">weekly site audit</a> ($50 a month
    per site) runs these checks every day on our servers, using each
    property's full 2,000 checks. Your agent's <code>seo index</code> then
    answers at once from the stored results, and you get an email when a
    page drops out of Google's index or comes back. <a href="/docs/alerts">Alerts</a>{' '}
    covers each email.
  </p>
</FeaturePage>
